The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
descr->skb = netdev_alloc_skb(*card->netdev, rx_skb_size);
descr->skb = NULL;
```
Reason:
The SKB pointer (`descr->skb`) is set to NULL immediately after it is allocated, which means that when the code later attempts to access `descr->skb`, it is referencing a NULL pointer, leading to a kernel panic.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
descr->skb = netdev_alloc_skb(*card->netdev, rx_skb_size);
if (!descr->skb) {
    descr->hw_regs.payload.dev_addr = 0; /* tell DMAC don't touch memory */
    return -ENOMEM;
}
```
Reason:
The initialization of `descr->skb` is now correctly sequenced before any access to it, ensuring that it points to a valid allocated SKB. The check for allocation failure is also retained, preventing further access to a NULL pointer if the allocation fails.

